In this lesson, we'll look at the three fundamental laws that govern how exponents work, even when variables are involved. Don't worry, there are really only three rules to remember, and they all feel pretty natural once you see them.

The first law is the product rule. When you multiply two powers with the same base, you simply add the exponents together. So x cubed times x to the fifth becomes x to the eighth, because three plus five is eight.

The second law is the quotient rule. When you divide two powers with the same base, you subtract the exponents. So x to the tenth divided by x to the fourth becomes x to the sixth.

The third law is the power of a power rule. When you raise a power to another power, you multiply the exponents. So the quantity x squared, all raised to the fifth, becomes x to the tenth.

Now here's the good news: variables follow exactly the same rules. The only extra step is that you also multiply the coefficients. So two x cubed times three x to the fourth gives you six x to the seventh, because two times three is six, and three plus four is seven.

When you have two different variables, just treat each one independently. For example, x squared y cubed times x to the fourth y becomes x to the sixth y to the fourth. And if you raise the whole thing to a power, every variable inside gets multiplied by that power.

Now let's see it in action.